WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:04.480
The man’s bedroom hadn’t been silent for
over 100 years now.

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:10.000
The carpet had been worn down, a thick layer
of dust had gathered on every surface, and

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:15.849
the same trapped air had stagnated for so
long that it had become unbearable for anyone

00:00:15.849 --> 00:00:17.080
entering the room.

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:20.100
But no one was allowed to enter the room.

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:25.230
Nobody could open that door ever again, or
it could mark the beginning of not just an

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:30.599
apocalypse, but a breakdown in the nature
of reality itself.

00:00:30.599 --> 00:00:32.770
Figures shuffled around the room.

00:00:32.770 --> 00:00:38.510
Sometimes dozens, sometimes as few as three,
all rotting but never dying.

00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:42.539
Researchers peered through the windows in
abject terror, day after day, as the laws

00:00:42.539 --> 00:00:46.219
of physics broke down before their very eyes.

00:00:46.219 --> 00:00:52.699
All of them except one, a lone scientist with
a plan to get inside that room.

00:00:52.699 --> 00:00:55.730
A plan that the Foundation would shoot him
dead for.

00:00:55.730 --> 00:01:00.910
A plan that he was seconds away from enacting
as the improvised explosive device in his

00:01:00.910 --> 00:01:04.289
satchel clunked heavily against his thigh.

00:01:04.289 --> 00:01:05.289
*

00:01:05.289 --> 00:01:09.810
In many ways, we are just as trapped as SCP-001,
if not more so.

00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:13.100
We are trapped in our own three-dimensional
world.

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:15.790
Take a stick figure drawn on a piece of paper.

00:01:15.790 --> 00:01:20.009
That stick figure knows up and down, left
and right, that is their world.

00:01:20.009 --> 00:01:25.350
You draw a box around them, and they are trapped,
unable to simply step forward out of the page

00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:27.000
and walk around it.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:31.600
For us, looking down at the stick figure,
we might laugh at their foolishness, trapped

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:34.000
inside a child’s drawing of a house.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:38.240
It begs the question, is there a creature
looking down at us and laughing?

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:43.560
We may be able to move up and down, left and
right, forward and backward, outsmarting the

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:45.190
simple stick figure.

00:01:45.190 --> 00:01:50.880
But in time, we are just as caged in, living
our entire lives on this razor-thin piece

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:53.040
of paper called the present.

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:58.130
The future is always just out of reach, the
past always repelling us, we have no choice

00:01:58.130 --> 00:02:04.409
but to exist in the now, slowly creeping through
history without any agency of our own.

00:02:04.409 --> 00:02:08.700
Cursed to be born, live, and die, in that
order.

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:12.920
You can never be a child again, never see
your mother’s face for the first time beaming

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:14.150
down at you.

00:02:14.150 --> 00:02:19.170
You cannot skip ahead to your dying minutes,
know what it is you will have to face up to

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:20.250
in your life.

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:25.410
Maybe the idea of facing up to things at all
will fade with the freedom of exploring what

00:02:25.410 --> 00:02:30.970
once was and what will be as if it were no
more difficult than taking a step out of a

00:02:30.970 --> 00:02:32.219
2D box.

00:02:32.219 --> 00:02:33.219
*

00:02:33.219 --> 00:02:34.760
The bell rang, cutting Professor Davies off.

00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:40.730
He hadn’t meant to go into such an existential
tangent, especially for his first-year students.

00:02:40.730 --> 00:02:45.340
Most of them looked like they were in a daze,
not really listening to anything he’d been

00:02:45.340 --> 00:02:46.340
saying.

00:02:46.340 --> 00:02:51.130
Quantum mechanics sure was a complex topic
to cover in just one semester at university.

00:02:51.130 --> 00:02:56.170
He’d spent his entire career trying to wrap
his head around it and still spent more of

00:02:56.170 --> 00:03:01.440
his days with the same bored and confused
glaze over his eyes as the teenagers who made

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:03.840
their way to the back of the hall.

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:09.370
But one figure, standing just beyond the doorway,
peering down at him, caught the Professor’s

00:03:09.370 --> 00:03:10.370
eye.

00:03:10.370 --> 00:03:15.299
Little did he know how drastically his career
was about to change in just a few minutes.

00:03:15.299 --> 00:03:20.290
A preeminent physicist at just 28 years old,
Professor Davies had already achieved the

00:03:20.290 --> 00:03:23.959
status that many academics will not see within
their lifetimes.

00:03:23.959 --> 00:03:27.970
Whenever anyone asked him about it, he always
made the joke that he was cheating.

00:03:27.970 --> 00:03:32.830
He didn’t believe in the concept of a linear
lifetime in the first place.

00:03:32.830 --> 00:03:38.150
Still too young to be taken seriously by many
of his peers, he had flown under the academic

00:03:38.150 --> 00:03:41.010
radar for much of his career so far.

00:03:41.010 --> 00:03:46.879
Perhaps it was as simple as that, or perhaps
certain undercover agents had a hand in suppressing

00:03:46.879 --> 00:03:50.379
news of his blossoming career in quantum research.

00:03:50.379 --> 00:03:51.379
*

00:03:51.379 --> 00:03:53.360
His hands trembled as he approached the reinforced
glass.

00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:57.720
He gripped the bag tightly, trying his best
not to let his fear show.

00:03:57.720 --> 00:04:02.620
If any of them saw what he was about to do,
if they even suspected something, he’d have

00:04:02.620 --> 00:04:06.170
a dozen bullets flying through the back of
his skull before he’d even have a chance

00:04:06.170 --> 00:04:07.689
to put his hands up.

00:04:07.689 --> 00:04:12.269
Shifting his weight from foot to foot, he
waited for the airlock to be opened for him.

00:04:12.269 --> 00:04:17.250
A siren blared, red lights flashed, and the
first glass door slid open.

00:04:17.250 --> 00:04:21.450
Stepping inside, he waited for the atmosphere
in the chamber to adjust.

00:04:21.450 --> 00:04:24.710
Both doors were closed in front and behind
him.

00:04:24.710 --> 00:04:28.170
If they wanted to kill him, now would be the
time.

00:04:28.170 --> 00:04:32.979
He closed his eyes and waited with bated breath,
the bomb weighing heavily on his shoulder.

00:04:32.979 --> 00:04:34.850
But they wouldn’t kill him.

00:04:34.850 --> 00:04:36.160
He knew that now.

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:39.219
What he was about to do… was inevitable.

00:04:39.219 --> 00:04:40.219
*

00:04:40.219 --> 00:04:43.009
It took several months for Professor Davies
to be granted the full security clearance

00:04:43.009 --> 00:04:44.009
he needed.

00:04:44.009 --> 00:04:48.520
They kept referring to his project as a K-class
anomaly, whatever that meant.

00:04:48.520 --> 00:04:52.690
In order to get him up to speed with the nature
of what he was to be working on, the Foundation

00:04:52.690 --> 00:04:56.740
assigned him to various other SCPs first.

00:04:56.740 --> 00:05:04.330
SCP-5027, 2821, 1887, any instance that had
some link to quantum mechanics or complex

00:05:04.330 --> 00:05:06.080
theoretical physics.

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:10.699
They wouldn’t even tell him which SCP he
was being trained up to work on until one

00:05:10.699 --> 00:05:14.500
morning when a team of agents was waiting
for him at the door to his quarters.

00:05:14.500 --> 00:05:18.550
He wasn’t allowed to bring a bag or any
personal possessions.

00:05:18.550 --> 00:05:23.430
Standard-issue clothing, a toothbrush, and
everything else he needed would all be provided

00:05:23.430 --> 00:05:24.430
for him.

00:05:24.430 --> 00:05:29.569
It was only then they told him which SCP he
was being assigned to.

00:05:29.569 --> 00:05:31.120
SCP-001.

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:33.510
He was blindfolded for the entirety of the
journey.

00:05:33.510 --> 00:05:40.360
Car, then plane, then another car, it was
a full 10 hours before his blindfold was removed

00:05:40.360 --> 00:05:43.030
and his new project was revealed to him.

00:05:43.030 --> 00:05:46.440
Professor Davies found himself in Site 01.

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:52.830
The place where all of this started, all the
SCPs, all the research, the secrecy, the assassinations,

00:05:52.830 --> 00:05:57.590
the deaths of countless innocent people, and
an equal number of guilty parties.

00:05:57.590 --> 00:06:01.470
It all started right here, Site 01.

00:06:01.470 --> 00:06:06.020
The site itself resembled a large sarcophagus,
similar to the one found covering the Chernobyl

00:06:06.020 --> 00:06:10.800
nuclear power plant - where do you think the
soviets got the blueprints for that from?

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:14.120
Everything in the room is arranged in a circle
around the center.

00:06:14.120 --> 00:06:17.930
Research desks, maintenance equipment, raw
materials including a stockpile of wooden

00:06:17.930 --> 00:06:24.290
planks and reinforced metal sheets, monitoring
equipment, security cameras, and of course,

00:06:24.290 --> 00:06:28.139
some of the most advanced automatic weapons
systems in the world.

00:06:28.139 --> 00:06:33.080
All pointed at a great glass box in the middle
of the hangar.

00:06:33.080 --> 00:06:38.650
Measuring 30m in each direction, the glass
was 50cm thick, even all those high-caliber

00:06:38.650 --> 00:06:41.900
weapons would have had a hard time creating
a chip in that.

00:06:41.900 --> 00:06:46.759
But it wasn’t the glass box itself that
was so interesting, it was what was trapped

00:06:46.759 --> 00:06:47.810
inside it.

00:06:47.810 --> 00:06:54.240
A house inside a perfectly round glass bowl,
with no entrances or exits.

00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:58.160
Complete with a front lawn, sidewalk, porch,
and even a tree in the backyard.

00:06:58.160 --> 00:07:04.110
A regular American house, trapped in a box
like some kind of science experiment.

00:07:04.110 --> 00:07:07.669
There was some kind of transparent airlock
system at the entrance.

00:07:07.669 --> 00:07:11.020
On the other side of that was just a normal
walk up the garden path to the edge of the

00:07:11.020 --> 00:07:14.020
second glass bubble enclosing the house.

00:07:14.020 --> 00:07:18.340
But on closer inspection, Professor Davies
realized that it wasn’t actually your typical

00:07:18.340 --> 00:07:20.060
American house at all.

00:07:20.060 --> 00:07:21.650
The street lamp wasn’t electric.

00:07:21.650 --> 00:07:25.120
It was made of iron with little glass-paned
windows.

00:07:25.120 --> 00:07:26.250
An oil lamp?

00:07:26.250 --> 00:07:28.930
In fact, the house did look odd to him.

00:07:28.930 --> 00:07:33.669
There was no electric doorbell, but instead,
an actual physical bell system.

00:07:33.669 --> 00:07:38.080
The whole house looked out of place, not just
because it was in a box in the central hangar

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:43.539
of a 42,000-ton sarcophagus, but because it
was from another era entirely.

00:07:43.539 --> 00:07:48.110
You see, Site 01 was constructed around this
house.

00:07:48.110 --> 00:07:53.090
The glass box and bubble, the sarcophagus,
all of it was built around the already existing

00:07:53.090 --> 00:07:54.150
building.

00:07:54.150 --> 00:07:59.180
Beneath the foundations, a hundred meters
beneath the surface, is an immense metal plate

00:07:59.180 --> 00:08:03.050
that forms a perfect seal with the defenses
up top.

00:08:03.050 --> 00:08:08.380
The only way in and out of Site 01 is through
an immense reinforced door.

00:08:08.380 --> 00:08:13.120
But even all of these defenses will prove
utterly futile if anyone ever opens the thin

00:08:13.120 --> 00:08:17.180
wooden door to the bedroom on the top floor
of the house.

00:08:17.180 --> 00:08:22.300
That is the home and the prison in which SCP-001
is trapped.

00:08:22.300 --> 00:08:27.360
It is the job of the Foundation to make sure
that it remains trapped there indefinitely.

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:30.200
What is SCP-001?

00:08:30.200 --> 00:08:34.250
As Professor Davies made his way through the
airlock for the very first time and entered

00:08:34.250 --> 00:08:39.060
the site under the supervision of four of
the highest-ranking SCP staff alive today,

00:08:39.060 --> 00:08:42.990
he laid eyes on the entity itself for the
first time.

00:08:42.990 --> 00:08:46.990
Standing on a scissor lift parked outside
the front of the house, just inches away from

00:08:46.990 --> 00:08:51.269
the glass bubble, he was lifted high enough
to look through the window into what looked

00:08:51.269 --> 00:08:54.690
like a young boy’s bedroom on the top floor.

00:08:54.690 --> 00:09:00.060
Layers of dust coated the surfaces, black
and white photos sat in frames, antique children’s

00:09:00.060 --> 00:09:04.430
toys sat all around the walls, but Davies
didn’t notice any of them.

00:09:04.430 --> 00:09:08.740
He was too busy watching the men walking in
all directions.

00:09:08.740 --> 00:09:14.899
Unhurried but with purpose, well over a dozen
men walked in all directions across the carpeted

00:09:14.899 --> 00:09:15.899
floor.

00:09:15.899 --> 00:09:20.540
They were all identical, around 6ft, with
heavy rotting skin that seemed to hang off

00:09:20.540 --> 00:09:23.760
their limbs like it would a carcass left in
the sun.

00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:28.200
Human cadavers, milling about in a young boy’s
bedroom.

00:09:28.200 --> 00:09:33.110
Davies was so taken aback by their appearance
and behavior that it took him a long time

00:09:33.110 --> 00:09:38.860
to notice something truly strange, the reason
he was brought here in the first place.

00:09:38.860 --> 00:09:43.140
They were walking through the bed in the middle
of the room.

00:09:43.140 --> 00:09:48.080
Not just over it or around it, but straight
through it as if it wasn’t there.

00:09:48.080 --> 00:09:52.180
But wait, something else was wrong too, there
weren’t 12 of them in the room.

00:09:52.180 --> 00:09:53.890
It was closer to 20.

00:09:53.890 --> 00:09:56.220
Had he miscounted a moment ago?

00:09:56.220 --> 00:09:57.730
Then he saw it take place.

00:09:57.730 --> 00:10:02.450
One of the cadavers reached the edge of the
room and paused, as if in thought, at that

00:10:02.450 --> 00:10:04.339
moment it split.

00:10:04.339 --> 00:10:09.100
Not in half, but rather two versions of itself
emerged from where one had been standing,

00:10:09.100 --> 00:10:11.130
both walking in different directions.

00:10:11.130 --> 00:10:14.740
A thrill of excitement ran through Professor
Davies’ body.

00:10:14.740 --> 00:10:16.540
The uncertainty principle.

00:10:16.540 --> 00:10:19.149
It was there in action in front of him.

00:10:19.149 --> 00:10:24.200
He was looking at Shroedinger’s cat inside
the box and seeing both its corpse and its

00:10:24.200 --> 00:10:25.620
living self.

00:10:25.620 --> 00:10:32.440
Where uncertainty exists, this entity was
somehow embodying both possibilities in real-time.

00:10:32.440 --> 00:10:36.200
He watched the two selves walk around the
room, following different paths for several

00:10:36.200 --> 00:10:40.920
minutes until eventually they converged again
back into one being.

00:10:40.920 --> 00:10:45.360
He turned to the researcher accompanying him,
lit up with excitement, and asked what they

00:10:45.360 --> 00:10:46.390
were doing in there.

00:10:46.390 --> 00:10:49.529
The researcher’s face was grim when he replied.

00:10:49.529 --> 00:10:50.800
They’re pacing…

00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:51.800
Waiting.

00:10:51.800 --> 00:10:52.800
*

00:10:52.800 --> 00:10:53.800
The airlock door hissed open.

00:10:53.800 --> 00:10:58.730
He felt the stale 1800s air hitting his face,
perfectly maintained at 20 degrees.

00:10:58.730 --> 00:11:02.730
In front of him was just the garden path,
leading up to his goal.

00:11:02.730 --> 00:11:04.360
That glass bubble.

00:11:04.360 --> 00:11:09.600
Gripping the satchel, he set off up the path
slow enough not to attract any unwarranted

00:11:09.600 --> 00:11:10.759
attention.

00:11:10.759 --> 00:11:11.759
*

00:11:11.759 --> 00:11:15.650
Over the next few days, Professor Davies learned
everything that the Foundation had on SCP-001.

00:11:15.650 --> 00:11:22.800
It had been a human, once: a radical physicist
born in the late 19th century.

00:11:22.800 --> 00:11:27.580
Intent on kickstarting the next century with
a nuclear renaissance, the young man had created

00:11:27.580 --> 00:11:30.589
a working model for stable nuclear fusion.

00:11:30.589 --> 00:11:35.630
The potential ramifications were enormous,
free clean electricity on a mass scale with

00:11:35.630 --> 00:11:37.660
no harmful byproducts.

00:11:37.660 --> 00:11:42.029
His research was almost complete when a covert
private military group poisoned him with his

00:11:42.029 --> 00:11:44.350
own radioactive nuclear agent.

00:11:44.350 --> 00:11:48.500
The young man made it back to his house, where
he died in his sleep that night.

00:11:48.500 --> 00:11:52.329
Powerful military personnel and prominent
scientists who had been working alongside

00:11:52.329 --> 00:11:54.950
the young man knew immediately what had happened.

00:11:54.950 --> 00:11:58.480
Bursting into his home, they were about to
walk through his bedroom door and save him…

00:11:58.480 --> 00:12:02.130
when they saw the shadow of two feet standing
by the door.

00:12:02.130 --> 00:12:03.829
Then another pair.

00:12:03.829 --> 00:12:05.520
The young scientist had died.

00:12:05.520 --> 00:12:07.700
But he also hadn’t.

00:12:07.700 --> 00:12:12.920
Much like Schrodinger's Cat, the man was suspended
in a dual state of death and not-death.

00:12:12.920 --> 00:12:17.860
This shocking discovery led the group of people
present to form a rudimentary Foundation,

00:12:17.860 --> 00:12:22.620
established to secure and contain their once
friend within his room, never to be discovered

00:12:22.620 --> 00:12:24.210
by the wider world.

00:12:24.210 --> 00:12:29.490
Through close observation since this time,
it has been established that SCP-001 exists

00:12:29.490 --> 00:12:34.490
independently of linear time, in a kind of
superposition relative to the world around

00:12:34.490 --> 00:12:35.490
it.

00:12:35.490 --> 00:12:41.220
It is capable of occupying any space that
will or has been empty at any point in time.

00:12:41.220 --> 00:12:43.339
Say you have two chairs in a room.

00:12:43.339 --> 00:12:47.910
One chair has always been there, the other
was added to the room just five minutes before.

00:12:47.910 --> 00:12:52.160
The SCP is unable to walk through the chair
that was always there since it has always

00:12:52.160 --> 00:12:53.160
existed.

00:12:53.160 --> 00:12:56.810
But the chair that was added can be passed
through with no problem, because there was

00:12:56.810 --> 00:13:00.110
a point in time when that chair had not been
there.

00:13:00.110 --> 00:13:02.140
But it also works in the future.

00:13:02.140 --> 00:13:06.590
If a third chair was present, which had always
been there but was removed five minutes after

00:13:06.590 --> 00:13:11.490
the experiment, SCP-001 could pass through
that chair also.

00:13:11.490 --> 00:13:16.750
The Foundation theorizes - and hopes - that
the start of this ‘clock’ that SCP-001

00:13:16.750 --> 00:13:21.680
operates on was the point at which the young
scientist died and did not die.

00:13:21.680 --> 00:13:24.589
That is why it is incapable of escaping its
bedroom.

00:13:24.589 --> 00:13:30.089
And that is why it is of vital importance
that the bedroom door remains closed, forever.

00:13:30.089 --> 00:13:33.579
Ongoing maintenance work and preservation
techniques are being employed to keep the

00:13:33.579 --> 00:13:36.570
house in as stable a state as possible.

00:13:36.570 --> 00:13:41.260
Should anything happen to damage the integrity
of the structure, the quantum chaos contained

00:13:41.260 --> 00:13:46.449
in that space could lead to a total breakdown
of the laws of physics, destroying not just

00:13:46.449 --> 00:13:50.140
the world but the fundamentals of mathematics
as we know them.

00:13:50.140 --> 00:13:51.140
*

00:13:51.140 --> 00:13:54.550
The bomb bumped against Professor Davies’
thigh as he walked up to the house.

00:13:54.550 --> 00:13:59.130
An old man whose life had been devoted to
denying the world its future.

00:13:59.130 --> 00:14:04.300
A reality where what is and what could be
are just as real as one another.

00:14:04.300 --> 00:14:09.550
A world where the pain of living in the now
fades, where you can travel to any moment

00:14:09.550 --> 00:14:11.589
in your life and relive its joy.

00:14:11.589 --> 00:14:14.950
Where you aren’t trapped by the aging process.

00:14:14.950 --> 00:14:18.190
Infinite renewable energy to solve the world’s
problems.

00:14:18.190 --> 00:14:21.140
A chance to see your deceased loved ones again.

00:14:21.140 --> 00:14:24.750
A way to look at the universe through the
eyes of God.

00:14:24.750 --> 00:14:30.760
The head of 001 containment, he had spent
his entire career overriding all of the safeguards,

00:14:30.760 --> 00:14:36.300
tweaking containment rules just enough each
year, all so he could walk across this grass,

00:14:36.300 --> 00:14:41.940
with a satchel on his shoulder, and an explosive
more important than the atomic bomb at his

00:14:41.940 --> 00:14:42.940
hip.

00:14:42.940 --> 00:14:44.510
He approached the glass of the inner sphere.

00:14:44.510 --> 00:14:49.310
If he had done the math right, this should
do just enough damage to create a hole in

00:14:49.310 --> 00:14:50.950
the containment structure.

00:14:50.950 --> 00:14:55.730
The pressure change wouldn’t be too great
inside, but should be just enough to blow,

00:14:55.730 --> 00:14:59.640
say, a bedroom door wide open…

